# Deliberate binary breaks in 1.13.0, relative to 1.12.0. See the release note in README.md.
#
# THESE FILTERS DO NOT RETIRE. sbt-mima reads every *.backwards.excludes in this directory
# whatever it is named, so this file stays in force until somebody deletes it. Delete it at the
# 1.13.0 post-release, when previousRelease moves to 1.13.0. See #107, where three stale files
# had put 81 filters in force where the build comment implied 36.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# #102: VertexMap.logger is deleted.
#
# It was public, so it was published API, which is why its own scaladoc said it was queued for
# the next breaking window rather than removed in 1.12.x. Nothing ever logged through it, and
# after #57 turned the orientation warning into a Failure there is no logging call anywhere in
# src/main, so slf4j-api went with it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# #130: Kruskal.mst and Boruvka.mst return the edge-keyed TraversalResult, not a bare Seq.
#
# An MST edge list in order of addition is exactly what the edge-keyed branch is for: positional,
# with no meaningful vertex key, and an order that means something (ascending weight for Kruskal,
# round order for Boruvka). Prim's result is a parent map and stays vertex-keyed, so the two
# branches of TraversalResult now express a real distinction rather than one being decoration.
#
# This also settles the circular justification #71 rested on, that the branch was alive because
# edgeTraversal populated it and one spec read it. It now has a producer in src/main.
#
# The FinalMethodProblem on `size` is a consequence rather than a decision: the branch extends
# Seq to stay usable -- 46 call sites in the suite alone wanted map, exists, head or sorted -- and
# SeqOps declares `size` final, defined as `length`. No caller can tell the difference.
#
# The lines below are MiMa's own suggestions from the failing run, not hand-written.

ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.VertexMap.logger()org.slf4j.Logger")
ProblemFilters.exclude[FinalMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.AbstractEdgeTraversalResult.size()Int")
ProblemFilters.exclude[IncompatibleResultTypeProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.Boruvka.mst(com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.adjunct.UndirectedGraph,cats.kernel.Monoid,scala.math.Ordering)scala.collection.immutable.Seq")
ProblemFilters.exclude[IncompatibleResultTypeProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.Kruskal.mst(com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.adjunct.UndirectedGraph,scala.math.Ordering)scala.collection.immutable.Seq")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# #135: serialize takes a Parseable[Z], to render the edge type.
#
# It used to write `source,target,Some(5.0)` -- comma-separated where the parser wants
# whitespace, Scala's Option.toString where it wants a bare number, and NO edge type at all, so
# directedness was lost. Nothing could read back what the library wrote, and the only test was
# `serialize should not be empty`.
#
# Rendering Z needs an instance, because Z is generic and EdgeType is the one type here whose
# toString is wrong for the parser: "Directed" where it reads ">". Parseable carries `render` as
# the inverse of `parse`, defaulting to toString, and ParseableEdgeType overrides it.
#
# Nothing called serialize outside a test, and what it emitted could not be parsed, so there is
# no working behaviour being broken here -- only a signature.
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.ProtoConnexions.serialize()java.lang.String")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.SerializableGraph.serialize()java.lang.String")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.Triplets.serialize()java.lang.String")
